# The project registry baked into the deployed worker (OPENFACTORY_REGISTRY points here).
#
# COPY THIS TO deploy/registry.yaml AND FILL IN YOUR PROJECT(S). The real file is
# gitignored (it holds your deployment's project coordinates) — this .example is
# the tracked template. deploy.sh fails loud if deploy/registry.yaml is missing.
#
# repo_path is a placeholder — a remote box clones the repo fresh from the forge, so
# only tracker/forge repo + board matter here. Register more projects by adding entries.
#
# THE CHANNEL: no key here, and that is the working configuration. Unset means the PANEL, the
#   surface that always exists. A chat channel is an ADD-ON PACKAGE, and its coordinates
#   (`channel_id`, `admins`, `channel_options.bot_token_env` / `.app_token_env` — all of them
#   still accepted under their old `slack_*` spellings, `ProjectRegistry._RENAMED`) select that
#   package's kind: on a deployment that has not installed it, a project carrying one is refused
#   by name when its channel is built, and only that project loses its chat. Add them once the
#   package is there, with the variables its README names — one workspace, channel and bot per
#   project.
#
# language: en (default) — the tech-lead's and product role's DEFAULT language, used only
#   when an agent speaks FIRST (an announcement, a diagnosis, an unprompted question). A reply
#   always mirrors whatever language the human just wrote in, regardless of this setting — it
#   never overrides a real answer, only the cold-start case where there is nothing to mirror
#   yet. Set it here, not in .openfactory/project.yaml: it is about how the factory talks to THIS
#   project's humans, the same axis as the channel above, not about the code.
#
#   THE DEFAULT IS ENGLISH AND THIS FILE SAID pt-BR (#160). It was true of nothing: the code's
#   `DEFAULT_LANGUAGE` has been `en` for as long as the field has existed, and the composers that
#   ignored the setting altogether wrote Brazilian Portuguese — so a reader of this file, a reader
#   of the code and a reader of the CHANNEL each got a different answer. A client who speaks
#   something else is NAMED here rather than assumed; that is what the field is for.
projects:
  myapp:
    name: myapp
    repo_path: /work/myapp
    enabled: false   # flip to true AFTER pointing tracker/forge at a real repository — a
                     # phantom enabled project is polled every few minutes with your credential
    manifest_path: .openfactory/project.yaml
    language: pt-BR   # this example project is Brazilian; yours says whatever your client reads
    # Where the FACTORY files its OWN impediments — never the client's board (ADR-0027).
    # Absent, an impediment becomes a log line (`OPENFACTORY_OPS_NO_BOARD`) and reaches nobody, while the
    # product agent tells the client the team was warned. `supervisor` is the forge login of who
    # is accountable; empty is allowed and says so loudly on every ticket it files.
    factory_board:
      tracker:
        kind: github
        repo: yourorg/your-openfactory-platform-fork
      supervisor: yourlogin
    tracker:
      kind: github
      repo: yourorg/myapp
      options:
        board_owner: yourorg
        board_number: '1'
    forge:
      kind: github
      repo: yourorg/myapp
      options:
        board_owner: yourorg
        board_number: '1'
    ci:
      kind: github
      repo: yourorg/myapp
      options:
        board_owner: yourorg
        board_number: '1'
